Build your first actor 
 An event-sourced actor turns a command into an event and rebuilds its in-memory state by applying events. This counter is deliberately small so the roles are visible. The complete snippet was compiled in a fresh .NET 10 project against the packaged Troolio.Core 10.0.6 and Orleans 10 SDK. 
 Define messages and the actor 
 using Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration;
using Orleans;
using Troolio.Core;
using Troolio.Stores;

namespace MyApp;

public interface ICounterActor : IActor { }

[GenerateSerializer]
public record Increment(Metadata Headers) : Command<ICounterActor>(Headers);

[GenerateSerializer]
public record Incremented(Metadata Headers) : Event(Headers);

[GenerateSerializer]
public record CurrentCount : Query<ICounterActor, int>;

public sealed class CounterActor(IStore store, IConfiguration configuration)
    : EventSourcedActor(store, configuration), ICounterActor
{
    private int count;

    public IEnumerable<Event> Handle(Increment command) =>
        [new Incremented(command.Headers)];

    public void On(Incremented _) => count++;

    public int Handle(CurrentCount _) => count;
}

 Increment  is a command.  Handle  emits an  Incremented  event.  On  applies the event both after an accepted append and during replay when the actor activates again.  CurrentCount  reads the actor's current state. The actor's event stream, not the  count  field, is the durable write history when a durable store is configured. 
 Metadata  carries correlation, user, device, and message IDs. In a serving application, the host must bind the user and target to the current authenticated principal. Caller supplied headers alone are not authority. 
 Compile the example 
 Add the snippet as  Counter.cs  in a  net10.0  class library that references  Troolio.Core  10.0.6 and  Microsoft.Orleans.Sdk  10.0.0, then run: 
 dotnet build -c Release

 The source branch's package consumer proves the package installs; the actor example above was also compiled separately with zero warnings and errors. The snippet describes actor behavior, not a complete runnable silo.  Host setup  shows the next wiring step. 
 Next decisions 

 Choose an  event store and serializer . The bundled in-memory store is for local learning and tests. 
 Choose how to  project events into read models . 
 Decide whether the ordinary command path is sufficient or the  selected atomic route  is required. 


 Source note for maintainers:   Tests/CoreFunctionality/WidgetActor.cs ,  Tests/CoreFunctionality/SimpleActor.cs ,  Troolio.Core/EventSourcedActor.cs , and  Troolio.Core/Messages.cs  ground the example. The compiled verification source is at  /tmp/troolio-docs-counter/Counter.cs  in the release workspace.

Configure a host 
 A Troolio host composes Microsoft Orleans with the actor assembly, event store, serializer, and application services. The basic  TroolioServer  overload shown here compiles against Troolio.Core 10.0.6; it is a starting point for local evaluation. 
 Register actors and storage 
 using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Troolio.Core;
using Troolio.Core.Serialization;
using Troolio.Stores;

namespace MyApp;

public static class CounterHost
{
    public static IHost Build() => Host.CreateDefaultBuilder()
        .TroolioServer<InMemoryStore, JsonEventSerializer>(
            appName: "Counter",
            registerAssemblies: [typeof(CounterActor).Assembly],
            configureDelegates: _ => { },
            builderDelegates: null,
            disableActors: null);
}

 registerAssemblies  tells the host where to discover actor and projection types. The generic parameters choose an  IStore  and an  IEventSerializer .  configureDelegates  is where the application registers its own services.  TroolioServer  builds an  IHost ; the caller still starts and stops it according to the application's lifetime. 
 Choose a real deployment profile 
 The basic overload retains the bundled Local/Azure profile. An overload with  configureClustering  gives the application control over Orleans membership, endpoints, storage, reminders, and streams. When you use that callback, supply  all  required Orleans services for your deployment. This callback does not replace the domain event store or historical replay. 
 The in-memory store does not survive process exit. For persistent operation, choose a compatible adapter and review its schema, credentials, transaction contract, projection workers, and recovery path. See  SQL Server ,  KurrentDB , and the  combined integration . 
 Serving contract 
 A host must provide current caller authentication and command authorization, trusted source and owner observations where needed, route authority, provider idempotency, retention windows, and recovery operations. A package reference or successful silo startup does not establish serving readiness. Use the  deployment checklist  to review these pieces together. 

 Source note for maintainers:   Troolio.Core/SiloExtensions.cs  defines the overloads and callback behavior.  Troolio.Core/Stores/InMemoryStore.cs  defines the local store. The snippet was compiled with the packaged 10.0.6 Core library.
